# ENLIGHTENED: THE ACADEMY ERA, BOOK 1, "THE KING AND THE CLIMB"

## Chapter Twenty-Two: Midpoint

*"****Every point earned is a door opened. No door opens on its own.****"*

*Points-economy signage, combat wing*

Sunday morning the hall runs at about a third and the trays come out cold because nobody's stood over them.

Denic has three of them. Not three trays, three of the flat bread rolls stacked on one tray with his own arm curled around the outside of it like somebody's coming.

"Nobody's coming," Fenn says.

"Somebody came last Sunday," Denic says.

"Nobody came last Sunday," Fenn says. "You put it down and forgot where you put it and then you found it on the wrong bench and decided that was a person."

"It was on the wrong bench," Denic says.

"It was on the bench you sat on before you moved," Fenn says.

Denic keeps the arm where it is.

Doyle's stack comes out and it comes out in two pieces now, which is new. He puts the thicker one face down and works off the thinner one.

"Fourteen," Doyle says.

"Twenty-two," Corw says, from somewhere behind me, not sitting down yet.

"It is," Doyle says.

Corw stops where he is.

"You've fought me on that every morning since you started counting," Corw says.

"You've been right every morning since I started counting," Doyle says. "I went and looked Thursday. The eight are still up."

Corw gets round the end of the bench and sits and squares the tray and doesn't touch it.

"Half," Corw says.

"Of what," Denic says.

"Of the year," Corw says. "Eleven days out. July second is the middle of it and after that there's less of it in front of you than behind."

"That's a Thursday," Doyle says.

"So," Corw says.

"So nothing gets posted," Doyle says.

"It isn't that kind of day," Corw says.

Fenn's got her chin on her hand and she's watching him the way she watches him.

"You've had that number since Friday," Fenn says.

"I've had it since Wednesday," Corw says.

"And you're bringing it out on a Sunday," Fenn says.

"I'm bringing it out now," Corw says.

"You don't sit on numbers," Fenn says. "You say them the same hour you get them."

Corw eats about three bites of something before he answers that, which for Corw is a long time.

"The half in front is worth five of the half behind," Corw says.

Nobody has anything for that immediately.

"Five what," Denic says.

"Five times," Corw says. "Everything this table has pulled out of this place since January, all of it together, every Friday and every graded exercise and every pattern any of us ever got right. That's the small half. The half that hasn't happened yet is worth five of it."

"That's not how halves work," Denic says.

"It's not that kind of half," Corw says.

"Then don't call it half," Denic says.

"It's half the days," Corw says.

Doyle has stopped on the notice in his hand and hasn't moved it since.

"Say how you got it," Doyle says.

"Twenty a win in January," Corw says. "A hundred and twenty now. Two hundred and forty in December."

"That's twelve against one," Doyle says. "That's not five."

"That's the last month against the first month," Corw says. "I did every month. All twelve, added up, and it comes out five."

Corw looks at him for a second.

"Then say that first," Doyle says, "because you said twelve and you meant five," and goes back to the notice.

I get one of Denic's rolls off the far side of the tray while his arm's on the near side.

"That's mine," Denic says.

"It was on the wrong bench," I say.

Petra makes a noise into her own sleeve and Ilyen doesn't look up from her tray at all, and Denic points at me with the fork and doesn't get anything out.

"That's three," Denic says.

"Two," Petra says.

"The arm counts," Denic says.

"The arm isn't anything anybody did," Petra says.

"The arm's the best part," Fenn says.

Reqa comes in late with her sleeves pushed and both hands already washed, which on a Sunday means she's been up and stopped.

"Rail," Reqa says, before she sits down.

"I've got it on the stand," I say.

"You've got it on the stand since when," Reqa says.

"Since last night," I say.

"Then it's cold," Reqa says, and sits, and takes half of what's in front of me without checking what it is. "Good. Warm metal reads a different number than cold metal and the number I want is the cold one."

"I didn't know that," I say.

"You did it anyway," Reqa says.

Corw hasn't stopped.

"Nobody's said anything about the five," Corw says.

"You said the five," Fenn says.

"You said it back," Corw says. "That's not the same as saying something."

"What do you want said," Fenn says.

Corw opens his mouth and then doesn't use it, which I've seen him do twice in six months.

Petra leans forward across Ilyen's tray without moving it.

"What's it worth, then," Petra says. "Yours. Since January."

"Sixteen and a half," Corw says.

"And what's the five of that," Petra says.

"Eighty-two," Corw says. "Give or take."

"So you're carrying a hundred by December," Petra says.

"Ninety-nine," Corw says.

"That's a lot," Petra says.

"It's not," Corw says.

He says it fast, faster than he says anything else, and then he's got his hands flat on the wood on either side of the tray and he's looking at the middle of the table.

"That's the part," Corw says. "I did it Wednesday and I sat on it because I thought I'd got it wrong. A hundred thousand points and I went and read the whole wall in the combat corridor, all six of them, and I read the narrow two twice."

"There's six lists," Denic says.

"You've walked past them since January," Corw says.

"Four of them are the paths," Doyle says.

"And none of the four open until year three," Corw says, "which everybody knows, and which is not the part."

"Then what's the part," Denic says.

"The part is the two that are already open," Corw says.

Ilyen puts her fork down.

"The narrow ones," Ilyen says. "Both of them since February."

"They're in the eight," Doyle says. "They went up before I started counting."

"The first week of February," Corw says, "and there are two thousand of us and I have not heard one person in this hall say one word about either of them in five months."

I've walked past the corridor since February the same as everybody. Six boards under their own light, four wide ones and two narrow ones sitting together in the middle. The top of the third is a word I've had since about April and the rest is a column of names and a column of numbers. I've never stood in front of it long enough to get past the second row.

"What's on them," I say.

"One's reactors," Corw says. "The cheapest reactor on it is four hundred thousand, so forget the reactors."

"Then the other one," Denic says.

"Seven of them," Corw says. "It's not a path. Anybody can buy any of it from the first week, year one, because none of it's a weapon. It's the other kind."

"The other kind of what," Denic says.

"The kind that goes in you," Fenn says.

Denic stops with the fork halfway.

"That's not real," Denic says.

"Third from the left," Ilyen says.

"That's not what I mean," Denic says. "I mean nobody's got one. If that was real one of us would have one."

"Nobody's got one," Corw says. "That's what I've been carrying since Wednesday."

Doyle has folded the notice he wasn't reading and put it on the wrong pile.

"How much is the cheapest," Doyle says.

"A hundred and thirty," Corw says.

Doyle picks the notice back off the wrong pile and puts it on the right one and doesn't say anything else for a while.

"That's nothing," Denic says. "That's four rolls of wrap."

"It's less than four rolls of wrap," Fenn says.

"It's a hundred and thirty," Corw says, "and in January a hundred and thirty was seven bouts and I was getting three a Friday. So it was three Fridays. I looked at it in the first week of February and three Fridays was the rest of my life, and I decided it was a lot of money, and I have not looked at it since."

"So look at it now," Denic says.

"I did look at it now," Corw says. "That's the whole reason I'm sitting here saying it. A hundred and thirty is a bout and ten points. I took eleven off one Friday in May and any two of the eleven covered it, and I spent all eleven on a jacket and a pair of boots and a strap I already had."

"The wrap," Denic says.

"I'm not counting your wrap," Corw says.

Denic puts the fork down.

Petra has both elbows on the table now and her chin's nearly on the wood.

"What's the dearest one on it," Petra says.

"Seventy-six million," Corw says.

"Say that again," Denic says.

"Seventy-six million," Corw says. "It's the bottom of the board. A hundred and thirty at the top and that at the bottom, under the same light."

Petra sits back.

"Third one down is seventy-four thousand," Corw says. "It makes your rig hand you what it knows faster. That's the whole of what it says. Six words and a number."

"You said ninety-nine by December," Ilyen says.

"Twenty-five over," Corw says.

"You've got sixteen and a half," Petra says.

"I've got four hundred and six," Corw says.

Petra hasn't had that one before. I had it Friday, in the corridor, off him.

Reqa's been eating through the whole of it with her sleeves pushed and her hands flat when they're not moving, and she hasn't looked up once. She puts what's left of my bread down, wipes her thumb on the side of her trousers, and takes the last of it standing.

"Don't warm it up," Reqa says, at me, and goes.

Corw watches her the whole way to the door.

"She didn't say anything," Corw says.

"No," Fenn says.

I get up after her about a minute later. Denic's still got his arm round two rolls. Doyle has got both stacks out at once for the first time all year and is going down the thicker one from the bottom.

The bay's cold at the far end on a Sunday because the compressor's off and there's nothing running to warm it, and my rig's up on the stand with the seat out and the rail bare, which is not how I left it.

"You took the seat out," I say.

"At six," Reqa says.

"You said cold metal," I say.

"Metal doesn't get warm from being looked at," Reqa says.

She's got the gauge out of the drawer and she's got it flat on the bench first, on the ground edge, checking the zero the way she checked it Friday, and then she checks it again on the other end of the same bench.

"Twice," I say.

"The bench isn't flat," Reqa says. "The gauge is fine. I want to know how much the bench is lying to me before I put the gauge on something that matters."

"How much is it lying," I say.

"Not enough," Reqa says, and picks it up and comes over.

The rail runs the length of the seat mount and there's a groove worn along the inner face of it that I couldn't find with a finger in April and can find with a finger now. She puts the gauge into it at the top and runs it down and stops about two thirds of the way and holds it there.

"There," Reqa says.

"What am I looking at," I say.

"Read it," Reqa says.

There's a scale up the side of it and the needle's over on one side and there's a number under the needle and there's a smaller number under that. The digits come off it clean, the way the four on our own bench upright come off it now.

It's the mark that stops me. I've had the mark since May and I still have to work out every time which side of it does the small counting.

I take longer than the number's worth.

"A mark," I say.

"And," Reqa says.

"Then a six, then a three," I say.

"Point six three," Reqa says. "Millimetres. That's what the rail's lost."

"Is that a lot," I say.

"On a rail, no," Reqa says. "On a rail that's nothing, that's four months of your own weight going through the same place and it's exactly what four months should look like." She takes the gauge off and sets it down. "It's a lot on you."

"How," I say.

"Because the seat sits back point six three and your shoulder sits back point six three and the lance sits back point six three, and none of that matters," Reqa says, "except that your arm goes to where it remembers the tip being, and the tip isn't there anymore, and it hasn't been there since about April. So you go a finger past it. Every time. And a finger past it is a finger high, because you're going up and out, not straight."

"That's what you said Friday," I say.

"Friday I said it off you," Reqa says. "Now I've said it off the rail."

"You said it after Vrassik," I say.

"I said it after I watched your arm go a finger high nineteen times," Reqa says.

She gets the shims out of the drawer and they're already cut, four of them, which means she cut them before she'd measured anything.

"You cut those already," I say.

"I cut four," Reqa says. "I'll use two or three. I cut four because I didn't know which."

She gets the first one in behind the mount and puts the driver on it and stops.

"Sit in it," Reqa says.

"It's on the stand," I say.

"Sit in it on the stand," Reqa says.

I get up the ladder and get in and the seat's out so it's the frame under me, and the frame is cold in three places through the kit. She comes up the other side and gets her forearm across the rail and looks at where my shoulder sits against nothing.

"Put your arm out," Reqa says.

I put my arm out.

"That's high," Reqa says.

"It doesn't feel high," I say.

"You've said that," Reqa says.

She takes it in two goes and then a third. The third one she does with the seat back in and me in it properly. She doesn't ask me whether it feels different and I don't tell her, and it doesn't feel like anything at all.

"That'll hold to about September," Reqa says, from underneath.

"And then what," I say.

"And then it's worn another six tenths and I do it again," Reqa says. "Or the shims wear instead, which is what shims are for, and I put new shims in. That's cheaper than a rail."

"Cheaper how," I say.

"Cheaper in my Sunday," Reqa says.

She comes out from under it and gets the rag off her knee and works her thumb and the first two fingers, which don't come clean and haven't come clean since about March.

"The board," I say.

"What about it," Reqa says.

"You didn't say anything," I say.

"Meks don't get put in a square," Reqa says. "It makes a pilot's hand faster. I don't have a hand in a cockpit."

She puts the rag down over the open end of the housing on the bench.

"The other one I'd have said something about," Reqa says.

"The reactors," I say.

"Four hundred thousand is the right price for a reactor," Reqa says. "Everything you are ever going to want to do at once comes off the same line, and the line is the size it is."

"You could've said that at the table," I say.

"He wasn't asking," Reqa says.

"So the rail's fine," I say.

"The rail's fine," Reqa says.

"Me too."

Reqa's mouth goes at the corner and stays there and this time she lets it get most of the way before she puts it away, and she doesn't look up from the housing.

Denic's assessment is Tuesday at six and none of us see any of it, because it's bay four and bay four is the far side of the arena wing, and because six in the morning on a Tuesday is the rune block for the rest of us.

Doyle gets him down there at half five. I know that because I'm awake at half five and Doyle's already gone and Denic's bunk is already made, and Denic has never made a bunk.

"He did that Monday night," Fenn says at breakfast, and leaves it there.

The rune block runs and the panel puts up the set and my hand does the set. The half beat before each one is still there and it's longer than it was in April. I put it down to the hour, the same as I put it down to the hour in May. The machine goes up the aisle and doesn't stop at my desk.

Ilyen finishes before anybody. She sits with her hands flat and doesn't take the band off, and the machine goes past her twice.

"He's down there now," Ilyen says, without turning around.

"What," I say.

"He'll be in it now," Ilyen says. "He's two hours in."

"He'll be fine at two," I say.

Ilyen takes the band off and puts it square on the desk, the way she puts everything square, and says nothing for long enough that I have to ask.

"Say the rest of it," I say.

"There isn't any," Ilyen says.

"There is," I say.

Ilyen turns around in the seat.

"It's not the hip," Ilyen says. "Doyle's done the hip. The hip's the only part anybody's looked at since Friday and it's the part that was always going to be fine, because Doyle looked at it."

"Then what," I say.

"He talks," Ilyen says. "The whole time he's working. There's four hundred of us on a Friday and the only one who hears him is whoever he's fighting, so it has never once mattered. Tuesday there's six of them in a bay."

"And nobody minds," I say.

"And somewhere in the fifth hour he hears himself," Ilyen says.

"That's not a real problem," I say.

"It's a real problem for Voss," Ilyen says, and turns back around.

He comes up out of the arena wing at twenty past twelve and he comes up the stairs at the ordinary speed, and there's nothing on his face at all.

"Six hours," Corw says, from the top.

Denic doesn't slow down for it.

"Give me a number," Corw says.

"A number of what," Denic says.

"There's no number," Doyle says, coming up behind him. "It's a handling assessment. There's no score on it. There's a room and a time and now there's a room and a time that's finished."

"There's always a number," Corw says.

"There isn't one," Denic says, and goes past him.

He gets four steps down the corridor and turns around.

"They don't talk," Denic says.

"Who doesn't," Corw says.

"Third years," Denic says. "Four of them, and the one out of the other block, and me. Six of us in a bay for six hours and they said eleven words. I counted at the end because I had four hours to count in."

"You counted," Corw says.

"There was time," Denic says.

Corw doesn't do anything with that.

"Eleven's not nothing," Fenn says.

"Eleven's two of them saying good morning and one of them asking where the spare liners are," Denic says.

"And the fourth," Fenn says.

"The fourth one said my name at about five," Denic says, "and then said it was fine and I didn't have to answer that. That's the eleven."

"What'd you say to him," Petra says.

"Nothing," Denic says.

"Voss," Petra says.

"I said I'd been talking for five hours and nobody had told me to stop," Denic says, "and he said nobody was going to, and then he went back into it."

Denic stops.

"That's more than I meant to say," Denic says.

"It generally is," Fenn says.

"They're not being anything about it," Denic says. "That's the part. They weren't ignoring me. If they were ignoring me I'd have got it in the first hour and shut up. They just work like that. Six hours and nobody needs anything out of anybody, and that's just how the bay runs, and I was the only one in it who didn't know that going in."

"Doyle knew," Ilyen says.

Doyle doesn't say whether he knew.

"You could have said," Denic says.

"I did the seat," Doyle says.

"You keep doing the seat," Denic says.

"The seat was six hours," Doyle says. "I don't know what a third year's bay sounds like. I've never been in one."

Denic looks at him.

"All right," Denic says.

"Your hip," Doyle says.

"My hip's fine," Denic says.

"Say where," Doyle says.

"It's fine everywhere," Denic says. "The pads worked. I didn't notice them and then at about four I noticed I hadn't noticed them."

"That's what pads are," Doyle says.

"I'm doing it again," Denic says.

"There isn't an again," Corw says. "It's a rotation. It's six names and it's done."

"Then I'll get on it again," Denic says. "I'll get on it in August and I'll do eight."

"They don't do eight," Doyle says.

"Then I'll do the six twice," Denic says.

"That's twelve," Corw says.

"He knows it's twelve," Fenn says.

"He does not know it's twelve," Corw says.

The bay's warm by evening and both doors are wedged and the compressor's back on and cutting out every forty seconds, and Denic is asleep on the bench end at half seven with his boots still on, sitting up, which nobody wakes him for.

Doyle works around him. He's got the bracket back in the frame and he's got the four pads out on the bench again, and he goes over each of them with his thumb along the seam and puts three of them back in the box and leaves the fourth one out.

"That one's gone," Doyle says, to nobody in particular.

"After one," I say.

"After six hours," Doyle says. "It's a four-hour pad. It did six." He puts it flat on the bench and squares it to the edge. "I'll get another one."

"With what," I say.

"Two hundred," Doyle says. "That's not the middle board. That's stores."

He gets the box shut and then he stops with his hand on it.

"He'll do it again in August," Doyle says.

"Corw says there's no again," I say.

"There's an again," Doyle says.

Denic doesn't wake up for any of that.

Reqa's at our bench with a housing open and the rag over her knee and the driver behind her ear. She reaches for it once and finds nothing and doesn't say anything about it, and the second time she gets it, and she doesn't say anything about that either.

I sit down on the end of the bench. She doesn't ask me anything.

Down the aisle Petra's got her boots off and Ilyen's on the bench above her with her back against the frame. Petra says something into her own knee, and Ilyen takes about four seconds and then answers it, and Petra goes flat on the floor with both arms out.

"Eight," Denic says, with his eyes shut.

"Seven," Petra says.

"You're asleep," Fenn says.

"I count right asleep," Denic says.

Corw's on the far bench and he hasn't put a number on anything all evening.

He gets up at about eight and comes down the aisle and stops at the end of our bench.

"I've been at it since Sunday," Corw says.

"At what," I say.

"I'm going Thursday," Corw says. "Thursday there's the graded block and Friday there's the Fridays, and by Friday night there's a hundred and thirty in it and I'm going Saturday morning."

"You've got four hundred and six," Reqa says, from under the housing.

"I know what I've got," Corw says.

"That's not a part," Reqa says. "You can't put it back on the shelf if it doesn't fit."

Corw stands there for about as long as he stood there Sunday.

"It's a hundred and thirty," Corw says. "It's the cheapest thing on the board."

Reqa goes back under the housing.

"Right," Corw says, and doesn't go anywhere for a second, and then goes.

Reqa doesn't look up and doesn't say anything else about it, and about a minute later Fenn comes down the aisle after him with a coupling in her hand she isn't on the clock for and goes past our bench without stopping.

I get my kit off the hook and the wall panel by the door reads an hour and forty to lights-out.

"Straps," Reqa says.

"I've got mine," I say.

"I know," Reqa says.

The corridor down to the arena wing has both doors wedged at the bottom of it, and the woman at the desk has the slate up in front of her face and doesn't move it.

Sixteen tonight. I get it off the shape of the near squares before I'm through the doors, the same as every night, and the lights past the fortieth are off.

Up the ladder. Four straps. Twelve and stop, twelve and stop.

The fourth one goes through clean.

My arm's where it goes. I don't have to find it and I don't check it. The first one's over in about four seconds and the second one takes longer and goes the same way. Somewhere in the fourth I stop having a count of what my arm is doing and just have the count of them.

I get six.

The last one is somebody a year up who's been down here as long as I have and knows what I do with a back foot, and he waits me out for most of a minute before either of us moves, and then it's quick.

I come off with twenty minutes still on the lights.

The common room's down to about ten. Doyle's got both stacks out and he's putting the thin one into the thick one, one at a time, in an order that isn't the order they were in. Denic's awake and has the peg board flat on the wood and is running nine to five with the left, and the left hand goes nine, seven, five, and the middle of it is still where the middle of it is.

"Six hours in a cockpit and it hasn't moved," Denic says.

"That's a different hand," Ilyen says.

"It's the same arm," Denic says.

"It's a different hand," Ilyen says, and doesn't say the rest of it, and Denic starts again from the top and gets the same middle and doesn't stop.

Reqa's on the bench end with the housing shut and back in one piece and nothing in front of her at all, which she doesn't do.

"Six," I say, before she asks.

"Out of," Reqa says.

"Nine," I say.

"Nine's a lot," Reqa says.

"It felt like six," I say.

"That's what I said," Reqa says, and picks the housing up off the bench and turns it over once and puts it back down the same way round.

Corw comes in last with his jacket already off, and he goes straight past all of us to the far bench, and about halfway there he stops next to Denic.

"A hundred and thirty," Corw says.

Denic doesn't look up off the board.

"There's your number," Corw says. "The one you said doesn't exist."

"You said that Sunday," Denic says.

"And it's still true," Corw says, and goes and sits down.

I'm on the floor with my back on the bench end and Petra's boots are already where my leg goes, and she moves them without looking, and Denic runs the diagonal again and gets the same middle.
